          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 警车",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "警车 — jǐngchē — \"police car\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "小偷看到警车就走了 xiǎotōu kàndào jǐngchē, jiù zǒu le — The thief saw the police car and left."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 警 (jǐng) — nineteen strokes in three pieces: top left, the grass piece of 花, then a two-stroke wrap with 口 inside; top right, the striking piece of 救; across the foot a dot, three bars and 口. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "警车 (jǐngchē) is built from 警 (jǐng) 'to warn' and 车 'a vehicle', both inherited from Middle Chinese, kjaengX and tsyhae (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'to wake' and 'to guard against'."
            }
          ]
        },
